<h2>The transaction and the award</h2>
<p>In May 2019 the parties executed two agreements to sell for properties in DLF City Phase-I, Gurugram, each for Rs 5.62 crore, with Rs 5 lakh paid toward each. The buyer\'s ability to pay the balance rested on a loan sanction letter from an RBI-registered NBFC. The deal collapsed; the seller refunded the advance in February 2021 and the buyer invoked arbitration the same day, obtaining a stay from the arbitrator within days. Five years on, the award of 26 September 2026 produced a split result: the agreements were held valid, but the tribunal found the loan sanction letter to be a sham, held the buyer had not demonstrated readiness and willingness, refused specific performance and the injunction, and imposed costs of Rs 15 lakh. The buyer filed Section 9 petitions seeking to preserve the properties while it challenges the award under Section 34.</p>

<h2>The threshold for an unsuccessful party</h2>
<p>Section 9 permits interim measures after the award and before enforcement, but the position of a party that has already lost is structurally different: the award stands in its way. Relying on the Supreme Court\'s ruling in <em>Home Care Retail Marts v. Haresh N. Sanghavi</em> (2026 INSC 415), Justice Tushar Rao Gedela proceeded on the footing that an unsuccessful claimant must show rare and compelling circumstances before a court will freeze the subject matter pending the Section 34 challenge. The judgment then found them.</p>

<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>A finding on no evidence.</strong> The tribunal\'s conclusion that the loan sanction letter was a sham was prima facie unsupported: the document\'s authenticity was neither questioned nor discredited, and no challenge to its genuineness was ever put to the buyer\'s witness in cross-examination.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>A judgment-proof respondent.</strong> The seller appeared to have no assets other than the two properties — so a successful Section 34 outcome followed by fresh proceedings could prove worthless if the properties were sold meanwhile.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>A remedy money cannot replace.</strong> After the 2018 amendment to the Specific Relief Act, the grant of specific performance is a mandate, not a discretion; damages are no longer the default substitute for conveyance of specific immovable property.</div>
</div>

<h2>The balance the Court struck</h2>
<p>The operative order restrains the respondent from selling, alienating or creating third-party rights in the two properties until the next date, with a reply in three weeks, rejoinder in two, and listing on 2 December 2026. The Court reasoned that without protection, the efficacy of the Section 34 challenge would be rendered nugatory — the classic justification for preserving the res. It also distinguished precedent pressed by the seller on the ground that, unlike the litigant there, this buyer had asserted and evidenced its financial capacity to pay the price.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>The decision pairs instructively with the same week\'s Division Bench ruling in <em>Flowmore v. Skipper</em>: in both, the lever was a core arbitral finding that prima facie rested on no evidence. The lesson for arbitrations is procedural — a document the opposing side never confronts in cross-examination is very hard to call a sham in the award; and the lesson for post-award strategy is that such a defect, spotted early, can found not only the Section 34 challenge but interim protection that keeps the dispute worth winning.</p>
</div>

<h2>For transaction and dispute counsel</h2>
<p>Buyers under agreements to sell should treat this judgment as the current Delhi template for post-award preservation: plead the respondent\'s asset position specifically, establish financial readiness on the record, and move under Section 9 immediately upon an adverse award rather than waiting for the Section 34 hearing. Sellers and award-holders, conversely, should expect that an application to vacate or resist such protection will turn on evidence of other assets and on defending the tribunal\'s findings as evidence-based — and should remember that an interim restraint is not a reversal: the award, including its costs component, remains alive until the Section 34 court rules.</p>
